This chapter describes how the library states and proves the Reeh–Schlieder theorem for a
Haag–Kastler net in Minkowski spacetime. It corresponds to
[Section 10.9](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#sctn:reeh-schlieder)
of the blueprint. It builds on the {ref "haag-kastler"}[Minkowski chapter], which supplies the net,
its quasilocal algebra $`\mathfrak{U}`, the covariance action $`L \mapsto \beta_L` and the local von
Neumann algebras, and on the {ref "stone"}[chapter on Stone's theorem], which supplies positive
energy, the form in which the library states the spectrum condition.

# Overview
%%%
tag := "reeh-schlieder-overview"
%%%

As you will recall, the vacuum vector $`\Omega` of a quantum field theory is _cyclic_ for the
algebra of all observables: the vectors $`A\Omega`, with $`A` ranging over the quasilocal algebra,
are dense in the Hilbert space. Physically, every state of the theory can be approximated by acting
on the vacuum with observables. The Reeh–Schlieder theorem says something much stronger. For every
nonempty diamond $`\mathbf{B}`, however small, $`\Omega` is already cyclic for the local algebra
$`\pi(\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}))`: operations confined to an arbitrarily small region of spacetime
approximate, to arbitrary accuracy, every state of the theory, including states localised far away
in spacelike separation.

The theorem looks paradoxical and is not. It does not allow signalling, because the operators that
do the approximating are not unitary and their effect is not a physical operation that one observer
can perform with certainty. What it does show is that the vacuum is highly entangled across every
spacelike boundary. It has three consequences that the rest of algebraic quantum field theory uses
constantly:

* _Statistical independence._ The Schlieder property, which the Minkowski chapter calls
  statistical independence, says: if $`\mathbf{B}_1` and $`\mathbf{B}_2` are completely spacelike
  and $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}_1))`, then $`\Omega` is separating for
  $`R(\mathbf{B}_2)`. Its cyclicity hypothesis is exactly what the Reeh–Schlieder theorem supplies,
  so for the vacuum of a weakly additive net the property needs no separate cyclicity hypothesis.
* _The vacuum is separating._ Combining the two, no nonzero local observable of a region with
  spacelike room around it annihilates the vacuum: a local detector placed in the vacuum has a
  nonzero probability of clicking.
* _Modular theory._ A vector that is both cyclic and separating for a von Neumann algebra is
  exactly the input of Tomita–Takesaki theory. The Reeh–Schlieder theorem therefore starts the
  modular theory of local algebras, and through it the Bisognano–Wichmann theorem and the KMS
  property of the vacuum restricted to a wedge. None of these is formalized in the library; they
  are mentioned as context.

The library proves the theorem in two stages, following the blueprint.

The main theorem, `reeh_schlieder`, assumes no additivity property of the net. Its hypotheses are
a \*-representation $`\pi` of $`\mathfrak{U}` on a Hilbert space $`H`, a vector $`\Omega`, and
unitaries $`U(x)`, $`x \in \mathbb{R}^4`, that implement the translations on the vectors
$`\pi(a)\Omega` and have positive energy along every future-timelike direction. If a diamond
$`\mathbf{B}` contains every small translate of a diamond $`\mathbf{B}_1`, it concludes that the
vectors $`\pi(A)\Omega`, with $`A` in the algebra $`\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}_1}` generated by all
translates of $`\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}_1)`, lie in the closure of $`\pi(\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}))\Omega`.
In words: whatever you can reach from the vacuum with operators localised anywhere in spacetime,
built from translates of a small region, you can already reach with operators localised in a
slightly larger region.

Corollaries turn this into cyclicity, under three hypotheses of increasing strength:

* `reeh_schlieder_cyclic`: $`\Omega` is cyclic for every translation-generated algebra
  $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}'})`;
* `reeh_schlieder_cyclic_of_vonNeumann`: $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})` and $`\pi`
  satisfies the von Neumann form of weak additivity;
* `reeh_schlieder_cyclic_of_weaklyAdditive`: $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})` and the
  net is weakly additive.

Each concludes that $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}))` for every nonempty
diamond $`\mathbf{B}`. The vacuum form, `reeh_schlieder_cyclic_of_vacuum`, states the last of these
for the implementation and spectrum data of a vacuum state. The separating form,
`reeh_schlieder_separating`, concludes that $`\Omega` is separating for the local von Neumann
algebra of every diamond completely spacelike to a nonempty diamond.

Every statement of Section 10.9 is proved in Lean, with no `sorry`. The theorem and its
corollaries are in the namespace `Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler`, the holomorphic extension under
positive energy is in `Physicslib4.AQFT`, and the complex-analysis lemma on vanishing on an edge is
in `Physicslib4`.

# Design
%%%
tag := "reeh-schlieder-design"
%%%

The conventions of the {ref "haag-kastler-design"}[Minkowski chapter] carry over. A diamond is an
Alexandrov basis set $`I^+(p) \cap I^-(q)`, the predicate `IsAlexandrovBasisSet`; the local algebra
of $`\mathbf{B}` inside $`\mathfrak{U}` is the range of the embedding $`\iota_{\mathbf{B}}`; the
translate of a diamond is $`\mathbf{B} + x = \mathrm{translationSub}(x) \cdot \mathbf{B}`, again a
diamond; and $`\beta_x` is the action of $`\mathrm{translationSub}(x)` on $`\mathfrak{U}`. A
\*-representation is a \*-algebra homomorphism `N.quasilocal.carrier →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)`, and a
unitary is a linear isometry equivalence `H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H`. A vector is cyclic for a set of operators
when its orbit is dense, and the library states each cyclicity conclusion directly as `Dense` of
that orbit.

## The main theorem assumes no additivity

The cyclicity of $`\Omega` for a local algebra cannot hold without some hypothesis linking the
local algebras to the whole of $`\mathfrak{U}`: a net could assign to every diamond the algebra
$`\mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}`, and then nothing local reaches beyond $`\Omega` itself. In the literature
the link is an additivity property of the net. The library separates the analytic content of the
theorem from this link. The main theorem, `reeh_schlieder`, assumes no additivity at all; it
compares two sets of vectors that the net defines in any case, $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}_1})\Omega`
and the closure of $`\pi(\mathfrak{U}(\mathbf{B}))\Omega`. Additivity enters only in the
corollaries, and only to identify the closure of $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}'})\Omega` with all
of $`H`. A reader with a different additivity assumption can derive cyclicity from the main
theorem directly.

The main theorem also assumes nothing about $`\Omega` beyond the implementation condition. It
does not assume $`\Omega` cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})`, nor a unit vector, nor invariant;
invariance under the $`U(x)` follows from the implementation condition with $`a = 1`.

## The corollaries quantify over nonempty diamonds

The empty set is a diamond, $`I^+(p) \cap I^-(q)` for $`q` not in the future of $`p`, and Axiom 1
assigns it the trivial algebra $`\mathfrak{U}(\emptyset) = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}`. Every translate of
the empty set is empty, so $`\mathfrak{A}_\emptyset = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}` too. Weak additivity as
"$`\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}} = \mathfrak{U}` for every diamond" would therefore force
$`\mathfrak{U} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}`, and cyclicity of $`\Omega` for $`\mathfrak{U}(\emptyset)`
would force $`H` to be at most one-dimensional. The additivity conditions, the cyclicity hypothesis
of `reeh_schlieder_cyclic` and every cyclicity conclusion are therefore stated for nonempty
diamonds, through a hypothesis `B.Nonempty`. The main theorem needs no such hypothesis, since for
empty $`\mathbf{B}_1` it states only that $`\Omega` lies in the closure of $`\mathbb{C}\Omega`.

## Weak additivity is a hypothesis, in three forms

Weak additivity is not one of the Haag–Kastler axioms of the library, and it is not added to
`HaagKastlerNet`: it is a predicate on a net, assumed by the results that need it. The library has
three forms of it, from the weakest assumption to the strongest:

* _Cyclicity for the translation-generated algebras_: $`\Omega` is cyclic for
  $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}})` for every nonempty diamond $`\mathbf{B}`. This is the hypothesis
  of `reeh_schlieder_cyclic`. It is not given a name, because it concerns one vector and one
  representation and is used only there.
* _The von Neumann form_, `HaagKastlerNet.IsWeaklyAdditiveVonNeumann`: for one representation
  $`\pi`, $`\pi(\mathfrak{U}) \subseteq \pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}})''` for every nonempty
  diamond. It allows $`\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}}` to be smaller than $`\mathfrak{U}` provided it
  generates the same von Neumann algebra in the representation.
* _Weak additivity_, `HaagKastlerNet.IsWeaklyAdditive`: $`\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}} = \mathfrak{U}`
  for every nonempty diamond. It concerns the net alone.

They are related by

$$`\text{weak additivity} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{von Neumann form for every } \pi \;\Longrightarrow\; \Omega \text{ cyclic for every } \pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}}),`

the second implication under the assumption that $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})`.
The first is `HaagKastlerNet.IsWeaklyAdditive.isWeaklyAdditiveVonNeumann`, since every set lies in
its bicommutant. The second uses that the closure of $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}})\Omega`
contains $`\pi(\mathfrak{A}_{\mathbf{B}})''\Omega`, a consequence of von Neumann's bicommutant
theorem for a single vector, and hence the dense set $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})\Omega`. The three cyclicity
corollaries follow the same chain: each is the previous one composed with one implication.

## Positive energy one direction at a time

The textbook proof uses the spectrum condition in its joint form: the energy–momentum operators
$`P^\mu` have a joint spectral measure supported in the closed forward cone, so
$`x \mapsto U(x)\varphi` extends holomorphically to the forward tube
$`\mathbb{R}^4 + iV_+`. The library has no joint spectral measure for commuting unbounded operators,
and the proof does not need one. It continues analytically in one complex variable at a time, along
one future-timelike direction at a time.

For a future-pointing timelike $`n`, the one-parameter group $`t \mapsto U(tn)` has positive
energy, that is, $`U(tn) = e^{itA}` with $`A` positive and self-adjoint. Every matrix coefficient
$`t \mapsto \langle \chi, U(tn)\varphi \rangle` is then the boundary value of a function
holomorphic on the upper half-plane and bounded and continuous on its closure. If the matrix
coefficient vanishes on an open interval of $`t`, Schwarz reflection and the identity theorem make it
vanish for all $`t`. So a function $`x \mapsto \langle \chi, U(x)\varphi \rangle` that vanishes on
an open set $`W` vanishes on $`W + \mathbb{R}n`. Applying this to the four future-timelike vectors
$`e_0` and $`e_0 + \tfrac{1}{2}e_i`, which form a basis of $`\mathbb{R}^4`, spreads the vanishing
from any nonempty open set to all of $`\mathbb{R}^4`. The spectrum condition is used only in the
per-direction form that the library states, `IsPositiveEnergy` of each future-timelike translation
subgroup, so the proof needs no structure beyond it.

The group law of $`U` is needed in the spreading step, and the hypotheses do not include it: the
unitaries are only assumed to implement the translations on the vectors $`\pi(a)\Omega`. The library
proves that on the cyclic subspace $`K_0`, the closure of $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})\Omega`, the
$`U(x)` satisfy $`U(x + y) = U(x)U(y)` and intertwine $`\pi` with $`\beta`, and runs the argument
inside $`K_0`. Cyclicity of $`\Omega` is not assumed, so $`K_0` may be a proper subspace of $`H`.

## The vacuum corollary needs only implementation and spectrum

A vacuum state in the library (`HaagKastlerNet.IsVacuumState`) is a state $`\omega` together with
the existence of a representation $`(K, \pi, \Omega)` and unitaries $`U(L)` that reproduce
$`\omega`, implement the Poincaré action and satisfy the spectrum condition. The corollary
`reeh_schlieder_cyclic_of_vacuum` takes the representation and the unitaries as data and assumes
only the implementation and spectrum conditions, plus cyclicity of $`\Omega` for
$`\pi(\mathfrak{U})` and weak additivity. The state itself, and the condition that the
representation reproduces it, play no role. The statement therefore applies to the witnessing data
of every vacuum state for which $`\Omega` is cyclic for $`\pi(\mathfrak{U})`. From the
Poincaré unitaries it uses only the translations $`U(\mathrm{translationSub}(x))` and the
positive energy of the subgroups $`t \mapsto U(\mathrm{translationFlow}(n)(t)) = U(tn)`.

